        <p class="profile-detail-region">Culture · Americas</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Haudenosaunee</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Homeland</span><span class="info-stat-val">south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-lakes">Great Lakes</a>, in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> State, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawk</a> in the east to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-people">Seneca</a> in the west, with territory of over 75,000 square km in the 16th century; today seven reserves in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/six-nations-of-the-grand-river">Six Nations of the Grand River</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akwesasne">Akwesasne</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kahnawake">Kahnawake</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanesatake">Kanesatake</a>, and eleven reservations in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, in New York, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wisconsin">Wisconsin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oklahoma">Oklahoma</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">the League formed before European contact, likely between 1142 and 1660, by many scholars about 1450; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beaver-wars">Beaver Wars</a> from 1609; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-peace-of-montreal">Great Peace of Montreal</a> of 1701; the Tuscarora joined in 1722; the American Revolution and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sullivan-expedition">Sullivan Expedition</a> of 1779; the fight for recognition at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> in 1923; living nations today</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-law-of-peace">Great Law of Peace</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-council-qing">Grand Council</a> of chiefs; the power of the Clan Mothers; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiawatha-belt">Hiawatha Belt</a> and the Two Row Wampum; the longhouse; the Three Sisters of beans, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> and squash; the Thanksgiving Address; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/longhouse-religion">Longhouse Religion</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/handsome-lake">Handsome Lake</a>; lacrosse</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How we know them</span><span class="info-stat-val">Haudenosaunee oral history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-peacemaker">the Peacemaker</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiawatha">Hiawatha</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jigonhsasee">Jigonhsasee</a>, and the wampum belts; the writings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-de-champlain">Samuel de Champlain</a> from 1603 and other colonial records; Lewis <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-morgan">Henry Morgan</a>'s studies in the 19th century; archaeological dating of the 125 villages of 1500 to 1700; the work of historians and anthropologists such as Richter, Dean Snow and Elisabeth Tooker</span></div>
